'''tuch†''', ''n.'' An obsolete spelling of ''touch''.

'''tuck<sup>1</sup>''' (tuk), ''v.'' [< ME. ''tucken'', ''tukken'', also ''touken''; partly < AS. ''tucian'', pull, pluck, full (cloth); partly < MLG. ''tucken'', LG. ''tukken'', ''tokken'', pull up, draw up, tuck up, also entice, LG. also ''tuken'', wrinkle, as a badly made garment, = MD. ''tocken'', entice, = OHG. ''zucchen'', ''zukken'', MHG. G. ''zucken'', ''zücken'', draw in, draw together, shrug, etc.; a secondary form of the verb represented by AS. ''teón'' (pret. ''teáh'', pl. ''tugon'') = OS. ''tiohan'' = MLG. ''tien'', ''tēn'', LG. ''teën'' = OHG. ''ziohen'', MHG. G. ''ziehen'' = Goth. ''tiuhan'', draw: see ''tee''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''tow''<sup>1</sup>, ''tug'', ''tick''<sup>1</sup>, ''touch''. Hence ''tucker''<sup>1</sup>, ''tucker''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To draw close together; pull together. {{smaller|Specifically—(''a'') To thicken; full: said of cloth. Compare ''tucking-mill''. [Now prov. Eng.]}}
{{smaller block|Cloth that cometh fro the weuyng is nouȝt comly to were Tyl it is fulled vnder fote, or in fullyng-stokkes, . . . ''Ytouked'', and ytented.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), xv. 447.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''b'') To gather up; draw or pull up, or in any direction; draw into folds: frequently followed by ''up''.}}
{{smaller block|And you ''tucke'' nat your gowne rounde aboute you, you shall be daggled by yonde all mercy.
{{right|''Palsgrave'', p. 763.}}
They ''tuck up'' the skirts of their coats when they fight or march.
{{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 38.}}
<poem>
She ''tuckt'' her girdle about her middle,
And ranne close by his side.
</poem>
{{right|''The Knight and Shepherd’s Daughter'' (Child’s Ballads, III. 262).}}
<poem>
So, Dick Adept, ''tuck'' back thy Hair;
And I will pour into thy Ear
Remarks, which none did e’er disclose.
</poem>
{{right|''Prior'', Alma, iii.}}}}
{{smaller block|(''c'') In ''needlework'', to lay and sew tucks in: as, the waist was ''tucked'' lengthwise. See ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.'', 2.}}

readings.c1s3:
'''2.''' To press or crowd into a narrow space or compass; stuff; cram.
{{smaller block|I . . . carry pistols about me, which I have always ''tucked'' within my girdle. {{right|''Steele'', Tatler, No. 164.}}
They [footmen] would come to an honest labourer’s cottage, eat his pancakes, ''tuck'' his fowls into their pockets, and cane the poor man himself. {{right|''Macaulay'', St. Denis and St. George.}}
The little cushions ''tucked'' in around her spine were of silk-covered eider-down. {{right|''The Century'', XL. 269.}}}}
Hence—'''3.''' To pack in barrels. [Prov. Eng.]
{{smaller block|185 hogsheads [of pilchards] were ''tucked'' on Sunday. {{right|''Morning Chronicle'', Aug. 28, 1857. (''Encyc. Dict.'')}}}}
'''4.''' To gird; clothe tightly or compactly; hence, to cover snugly with wrappings, as with bedclothes or rugs.
{{smaller block|He departed from Blaase and com to Bredigan, and he was ''tukked'', and on his heede a felt, and bar a longe staff on his bakke, and he was sklender and lene. {{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), ii. 279.}}
A bonnie lasse she was, verye well ''tuckt'' up in a russet petticoate. {{right|''Greene’s Vision.''}}
The pigeons were snugly put to bed in a comfortable pie, and ''tucked'' in with a coverlet of crust. {{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 428.}}}}
'''5.''' To put into one’s stomach; eat: usually with ''in''. [Slang.]—'''6.''' In ''seine-fishing'', to gather or draw (fish) out of a seine by means of a tuck-seine which is shot inside of the seine.
{{smaller block|''Tucking'' the fish is the next operation, and this is performed with the tuck-sean, which we described as being very deep in the middle. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', IX. 254.}}}}

