'''7†.''' To pinch; nip; wound by the pressure of the finger-nail.
{{smaller block|If any of the Freshmen came off dull, or not cleverly [in speaking], some of the forward or pragmatical Seniors would ''Tuck'' them — that is, set the nail of their Thumb to their chin, just under the Lipp, and by the help of their other Fingers under the Chin they would give him a Mark which sometimes would produce Blood.
{{right|''Life of A. Wood'' (by himself), p. 45.}}}}

'''To tuck up.''' (''a'') To gather or draw up. (''b'') To string up; hang. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|I never saw an execution but once, and then the hangman asked the poor creature’s pardon, and wiped his mouth as you do, and pleaded his duty, and then calmly ''tucked up'' the criminal.
{{right|''Richardson'', Pamela, I. 141. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To contract; draw together.
{{smaller block|An ulcer discharging a nasty thin ichor, the edges ''tuck'' in, and, growing skinned and hard, give it the name of a callous ulcer. {{right|''Sharp'', Surgery.}}}}
'''2.''' To make tucks: as, a sewing-machine that ''tucks'' and gathers.

'''tuck<sup>1</sup>''' (tuk), ''n.'' [< ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1†.''' A garment tucked, girt, or wrapped about one; in the following quotation, a turban.
{{smaller block|Vpon his head a goodly white ''tucke'', containing in length by estimation fifteene yards. {{right|''Hakluyt’s Voyages'', II. 113.}}}}
'''2.''' In ''needlework'', a flat fold in a fabric, or in a part of a garment, fixed in place by stitches, and frequently one of a series laid parallel. {{smaller|Tucks are used either by way of decoration, or in order to dispose of extra material in a garment, with a view to letting it out as the wearer grows or as the fabric shrinks.}} '''3.''' A short pinafore. ''Halliwell.'' [Prov. Eng.] —'''4.''' In ''bookbinding'', a flap on one side of the cover, made to fold over the other side and tuck

readings.c2s2:
into a strap which holds it fast.—'''5.''' A kind of net.
{{smaller block|The ''Tucke'' . . . is narrower meashed, and (therefore scarce lawfull) with a long bunt in the midst.
{{right|''R. Carew'', Survey of Cornwall, fol. 30.}}}}
'''6†.''' A pinch; a nip. See the quotation under ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>, ''v. t.'', 7.
{{smaller block|If dull, nothing was given to him [the freshman] but salted Drink, . . . with ''tucks'' to boot.
{{right|''Life of A. Wood'' (by himself), p. 46.}}}}
'''7.''' ''Naut.'', that part of a vessel where the after ends of the outside planking come together under the stern.—'''8.''' Eatables; viands; especially, sweets or pastry. Also ''tucker'', in Australia. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|Nothing can stop the mouth of a ''tuck''-hunter.
{{right|''A. Bunn'', The Stage, I. 295.}}
The Slogger looks rather sodden, as if he didn’t take much exercise and ate too much ''tuck''.
{{right|''T. Hughes'', Tom Brown at Rugby, ii. 5.}}}}
'''9.''' An appetite. ''Halliwell.'' [Slang.]—'''Nip and tuck.''' {{smaller|See ''nip''<sup>1</sup>.}}

