'''tuck'''<sup>2</sup> (tuk), ''n.'' [< OF. ''estoc'', a rapier, also the stock of a tree, also a thrust (see ''tuck''<sup>3</sup>), = It. ''stocco'', a truncheon, short sword, tuck: see ''stock''<sup>2</sup>, ''stuck''<sup>3</sup>. For the form ''tuck'', < OF. ''estoc'', cf. ''ticket'', < OF. *''estiquet'', ''etiquet''.] A rapier. See ''estoc''.
{{smaller block|That wicked pernicious fashion to fight in the fields in duels with a rapier called a ''tucke'' only for the thrust.
{{right|''Darcie'', Annals of Elizabeth, quoted in Encyc. Brit., IX. 70.}}
Dismount thy ''tuck'', be yare in thy preparation, for thy assailant is quick, skilful, and deadly.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., iii. 4. 244.}}
Now with their long ''Tucks'' thrusting at the face, now with their piked Targets bearing them down.}}

