'''tuck-creaser''' (tuk′krē″sėr), ''n.'' An attachment

readings.c3s3:
to a sewing-machine which creases the fabric as it passes through the machine, in order to make a guiding line for the next tuck. It usually consists essentially of an adjustable spring-bar.

'''tucked''' (tukt), ''p. a.'' [Also ''tuckt''; < ME. ''tukked''; pp. of ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] Treated, affected, or arranged in any manner noted by the verb ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|A short ''tuckt'' garment of flame-colour.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', King James’s Coronation Entertainment.}}}}
{{smaller|'''Tucked up.''' (''a'') Having the clothes drawn up so as to clear the ground.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>The ''tuck’d-up'' sempstress walks with hasty strides,
While streams run down her oil’d umbrella’s sides.</poem>
{{right|''Swift'', A City Shower.}}}}
{{smaller|(''b'') Hung high in the stock, so that the top is above the pivots or gudgeons: noting large bells.}}
{{smaller block|It is difficult to set a much ''tucked-up'' bell tolling, though easy to keep it up afterwards.
{{right|''Sir E. Beckett'', Clocks, Watches, and Bells, p. 380.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c'') Contracted; narrow: as, a ''tucked-up'' room. [Colloq.] (''d'') Cramped. [Colloq.]}}
{{smaller block|If a man is riding an ordinary fifty-eight inch roadster, it is clear that a closely built fifty-eight inch racer will be noticeably too short in the reach for him, and he will feel that he is what cyclists call “''tucked-up'',” “cramped,” or “going short.” {{right|''Bury and Hillier'', Cycling, p. 189.}}}}

'''tucker<sup>1</sup>†''' (tuk′ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. *''tucker'', ''tokker'', ''touker'', ''towker'', ''tuker'', ''toucher'', a fuller, < ''tuken'', < AS. ''tucian'', pluck, pull, tease, full: see ''tuck''<sup>1</sup>.] A fuller.
{{smaller block|<poem>Wollene websteris and weueris of lynen,
Taillours, tanneris, & ''tokkeris'' bothe.</poem>
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (A), Prol., l. 100.}}}}

